    "result_text": "The suite is complete \u2014 `AcceptanceTest` is the only concrete test class (`AcceptanceBase` is abstract, `AuditLogCapture` is a helper). The build passes.\n\nThe change is implemented and green. Summary of what I did, following the existing `bulkSignupWarning` pattern:\n\n1. **`AssignCapacityWarning.java`** (new) \u2014 a pipeline function that snapshots the count of existing owners in the owner's city (case-insensitive, collapsed whitespace, matching `EnsureCityCapacity`) and sets `capacityWarning = true` when that count is 40\u201349 inclusive.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new `assignCapacityWarning` step into the create pipeline (inside the transaction, after `assignBulkSignupWarning`, before `assignPossibleDuplicate`), so it runs after `BuildOwner` populates the entity and before `SaveOwner`.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `capacityWarning` field (`@Column(name = \"capacity_warning\")`) with getter/setter.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `capacityWarning` (boolean, read-only) to the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name.\n\n5. **Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning BOOLEAN` column.\n\nThe hard rejection at 50 (`EnsureCityCapacity`) is untouched; since it runs earlier in the pipeline, the count seen by the warning step is always below 50. `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
